ing in Malwani. Gaav Gaata Gazali (Zee Marathi), a light-hearted TV serial that showcases village life, folk traditions, and daily humor, all in rich Malwani speech.[1]',NULL),(7692,'culture/images/maharashtra/ratnagiri/language/ratris-khel-chale-transl-the-game-runs-at-_FhTAQEo.png','Ratris Khel Chale (transl. The Game Runs at Night) is a Marathi-Malwani supernatural thriller television series that premiered on Zee Marathi on 22 February 2016. Set in Sawantwadi, the story follows the Naik family, who speak in Malwani and encounter a series of mysterious and unexplainable events.[2]','Ratris Khel Chale (transl. The Game Runs at Night) is a Marathi-Malwani supernatural thriller television series that premiered on Zee Marathi on 22 February 2016. Set in Sawantwadi, the story follows the Naik family, who speak in Malwani and encounter a series of mysterious and unexplainable events.[2]',NULL),(7730,'culture/images/maharashtra/ratnagiri/local-politics/image-no-caption-bde0104a.png','Image (no caption)','Image (no caption)',NULL),(7787,'culture/images/maharashtra/ratnagiri/markets/chipluns-main-market-stretches-along-the-ce_cKDfFvv.jpg','Chiplun’s Main Market stretches along the central road, with open-air stalls and built-in shops offering everything from vegetables and fish to clothing and medical supplies. (Source: CKA Archives)','Chiplun’s Main Market stretches along the central road, with open-air stalls and built-in shops offering everything from vegetables and fish to clothing and medical supplies. (Source: CKA Archives)',NULL),(7791,'culture/images/maharashtra/ratnagiri/markets/chipluns-fish-market-is-run-mainly-by-women_XnRr79f.jpg','Chiplun’s fish market is run mainly by women from fishing communities. (Source: CKA Archives)','Chiplun’s fish market is run mainly by women from fishing communities. (Source: CKA Archives)',NULL),(7794,'culture/images/maharashtra/ratnagiri/markets/chipluns-vegetable-market-has-fresh-produce_Qu8nNHE.jpg','Chiplun’s vegetable market has fresh produce stalls, run by local farmers and nearby traders. (Source: CKA Archives)','Chiplun’s vegetable market has fresh produce stalls, run by local farmers and nearby traders. (Source: CKA Archives)',NULL),(7797,'culture/images/maharashtra/ratnagiri/markets/friday-market-shukravar-bazaar-in-pirlote-i_67CrLHU.jpg','Friday Market (Shukravar Bazaar) in Pirlote is a weekly open-air market with sections for vegetables, fruits, dry fish, hardware, clothes, and toast-khari, attracting crowds from across the district. (Source: CKA Archives)','Friday Market (Shukravar Bazaar) in Pirlote is a weekly open-air market with sections for vegetables, fruits, dry fish, hardware, clothes, and toast-khari, attracting crowds from across the district. (Source: CKA Archives)',NULL),(7816,'culture/images/maharashtra/ratnagiri/political-history/a-petroglyph-with-a-complex-geome_yXGkRc7.png','A petroglyph with a complex geometric design, which lies in Niwali, Ratnagiri district.[1]','A petroglyph with a complex geometric design, which lies in Niwali, Ratnagiri district.[1]',NULL),(7818,'culture/images/maharashtra/ratnagiri/political-history/a-petroglyph-in-barsu-sada-ratnag_mgTI1Jt.png','A petroglyph in Barsu Sada, Ratnagiri district, which depicts what locals believe to be the Tarawacha Sada (Master of Animals). This interpretation comes from the imagery of animals surrounding the body of a human-like figure and offers some insight perhaps into the human-faunal interactions of the time. Source:Heritage: Journal of Multidisciplinary Studies in Archaeology[2]','A petroglyph in Barsu Sada, Ratnagiri district, which depicts what locals believe to be the Tarawacha Sada (Master of Animals). This interpretation comes from the imagery of animals surrounding the body of a human-like figure and offers some insight perhaps into the human-faunal interactions of the time. Source:Heritage: Journal of Multidisciplinary Studies in Archaeology[2]',NULL),(7822,'culture/images/maharashtra/ratnagiri/political-history/one-of-the-seven-megalithic-monum_59ep4kc.png','One of the seven megalithic monuments called \'menhirs\' discovered by Satish Lalit in the Kumbhavade village of Ratnagiri district.[3]','One of the seven megalithic monuments called \'menhirs\' discovered by Satish Lalit in the Kumbhavade village of Ratnagiri district.[3]',NULL),(7826,'culture/images/maharashtra/ratnagiri/political-history/parshuram-mandir-in-ratnagiri-sta_Lqt7fh5.jpg','Parshuram Mandir in Ratnagiri, stands at a site closely associated with the legend of Parshuram (the sixth avatar of Bhagwan Vishnu) and the origin of the Konkan Coast. (Source: CKA Archives)','Parshuram Mandir in Ratnagiri, stands at a site closely associated with the legend of Parshuram (the sixth avatar of Bhagwan Vishnu) and the origin of the Konkan Coast. (Source: CKA Archives)',NULL),(7829,'culture/images/maharashtra/ratnagiri/political-history/satavahana-settlements-in-the-wes_LTKRr5P.png','Satavahana Settlements in the Western Deccan. Source: Oxford University Press.','Satavahana Settlements in the Western Deccan. Source: Oxford University Press.',NULL),(7831,'culture/images/maharashtra/ratnagiri/political-history/map-showing-ancient-trade-centres_gKENjw7.png','Map showing ancient trade centres mentioned in thePeriplus of the Erythraean Sea(1st century CE), a Greco-Roman text describing maritime commerce in the Indian Ocean. The western coast of India is dotted with ports such as Barygaza (Bharuch), Suppara (Nala Sopara), Tagara (Ter), and Palaepatmai — the latter identified by some scholars with Palshet in present-day Ratnagiri district. This highlights Ratnagiri’s location within broader early trade circuits[4]','Map showing ancient trade centres mentioned in thePeriplus of the Erythraean Sea(1st century CE), a Greco-Roman text describing maritime commerce in the Indian Ocean. The western coast of India is dotted with ports such as Barygaza (Bharuch), Suppara (Nala Sopara), Tagara (Ter), and Palaepatmai — the latter identified by some scholars with Palshet in present-day Ratnagiri district. This highlights Ratnagiri’s location within broader early trade circuits[4]',NULL),(7834,'culture/images/maharashtra/ratnagiri/political-history/panhale-kaji-caves-are-a-group-of_Jn7OPZQ.png','Panhale Kaji Caves are a group of 29 rock-cut caves which are believed to have been first excavated by Hinayana Buddhists and later expanded under the Vajrayana tradition between the 3rd and 11th centuries CE.[5]','Panhale Kaji Caves are a group of 29 rock-cut caves which are believed to have been first excavated by Hinayana Buddhists and later expanded under the Vajrayana tradition between the 3rd and 11th centuries CE.[5]',NULL),(7836,'culture/images/maharashtra/ratnagiri/political-history/monolithic-jain-mandirs-at-konzar_nudNGAU.png','Monolithic Jain Mandirs at Konzar, Ratnagiri. Source: Bulletin of the Deccan College Post-Graduate and Research Institute[6]','Monolithic Jain Mandirs at Konzar, Ratnagiri. Source: Bulletin of the Deccan College Post-Graduate and Research Institute[6]',NULL),(7837,'culture/images/maharashtra/ratnagiri/political-history/an-image-of-a-tirthankara-at-dabh_cd4GHKD.png','An image of a Tirthankara at Dabhol, Ratnagiri. Source: Bulletin of the Deccan College Post-Graduate and Research Institute[7]','An image of a Tirthankara at Dabhol, Ratnagiri. Source: Bulletin of the Deccan College Post-Graduate and Research Institute[7]',NULL),(7844,'culture/images/maharashtra/ratnagiri/political-history/an-approximate-timeline-of-the-di_iJ7oeLI.png','An approximate timeline of the district. Note the overlap between the Angrias (1698–1756) and the Marathas (1660–1818). It should be noted that Angrias were part of the Maratha Confederacy – They were a powerful naval force under Kanhoji Angria, who declared semi-autonomy but still operated under the Maratha state. The chronology is based on information from colonial-era Gazetteers. (Source: CKA Archives)','An approximate timeline of the district. Note the overlap between the Angrias (1698–1756) and the Marathas (1660–1818). It should be noted that Angrias were part of the Maratha Confederacy – They were a powerful naval force under Kanhoji Angria, who declared semi-autonomy but still operated under the Maratha state. The chronology is based on information from colonial-era Gazetteers. (Source: CKA Archives)',NULL),(7852,'culture/images/maharashtra/ratnagiri/political-history/suvarnadurg-fort-near-harnai-ratn_6LejJSl.png','Suvarnadurg Fort, near Harnai, Ratnagiri. It wasoriginally built by the Adil Shahis to defend Dabhol and nearby ports from European and local threats.[8]','Suvarnadurg Fort, near Harnai, Ratnagiri. It wasoriginally built by the Adil Shahis to defend Dabhol and nearby ports from European and local threats.[8]',NULL),(7857,'culture/images/maharashtra/ratnagiri/political-history/alfonso-de-albuquerque-the-1st-du_mPxrf76.png','Alfonso de Albuquerque, the 1st Duke of Goa, after whom the famous Alphonso Mango is named[9]','Alfonso de Albuquerque, the 1st Duke of Goa, after whom the famous Alphonso Mango is named[9]',NULL),(7876,'culture/images/maharashtra/ratnagiri/political-history/gopal-krishna-gokhale10-f51d1ecc.png','Gopal Krishna Gokhale[10]','Gopal Krishna Gokhale[10]',NULL),(7878,'culture/images/maharashtra/ratnagiri/political-history/emergency-order-passed-in-ratnagi_2yATqSz.png','Emergency order passed in Ratnagiri during the Civil Disobedience Movement. Source: Azadi ka Amrit Mahotsav.','Emergency order passed in Ratnagiri during the Civil Disobedience Movement. Source: Azadi ka Amrit Mahotsav.',NULL),(7882,'culture/images/maharashtra/ratnagiri/political-history/emergency-order-passed-in-ratnagi_ukqnLln.png','Emergency order passed in Ratnagiri during the Civil Disobedience Movement. Source: Azadi ka Amrit Mahotsav.','Emergency order passed in Ratnagiri during the Civil Disobedience Movement. Source: Azadi ka Amrit Mahotsav.',NULL),(7891,'culture/images/maharashtra/ratnagiri/political-history/thibaw-palace-in-ratnagiri-built-_Kbirdaa.png','Thibaw Palace in Ratnagiri — built in 1886 to house King Thibaw Min, the last king of Burma, during his exile under British rule.[12]','Thibaw Palace in Ratnagiri — built in 1886 to house King Thibaw Min, the last king of Burma, during his exile under British rule.[12]',NULL),(7965,'culture/images/maharashtra/ratnagiri/sports-games/dilip-vengsarkar1-eb231217.jpg','Dilip Vengsarkar[1]','Dilip Vengsarkar[1]',NULL),(7985,'culture/images/maharashtra/ratnagiri/stories/the-thibaw-wada1-c97620d0.png','The Thibaw Wada[1]','The Thibaw Wada[1]',NULL),(7988,'culture/images/maharashtra/ratnagiri/stories/shyam-chi-aai2-730c0f54.png','Shyam Chi Aai[2]','Shyam Chi Aai[2]',NULL),(7991,'culture/images/maharashtra/ratnagiri/stories/konkan-petroglyphs3-3839daec.png','Konkan Petroglyphs[3]','Konkan Petroglyphs[3]',NULL),(7998,'culture/images/maharashtra/akola/architecture/entrance-of-the-kalanka-devi-mandir-built-_kTk9ctq.jpg','Entrance of the Kalanka Devi Mandir, built in black stone, showcasing the Hemadpanthi style and facing north. (Source: Purattatva)[1]','Entrance of the Kalanka Devi Mandir, built in black stone, showcasing the Hemadpanthi style and facing north. (Source: Purattatva)[1]',NULL),(8002,'culture/images/maharashtra/akola/architecture/the-entrance-of-the-mahadev-mandir-featuri_OPYkEps.jpg','The entrance of the Mahadev Mandir, featuring a brick and mortar wall (a later addition) painted in a faded pink color. (Source: YouTube @/RJ Dipak Wankhade)','The entrance of the Mahadev Mandir, featuring a brick and mortar wall (a later addition) painted in a faded pink color. (Source: YouTube @/RJ Dipak Wankhade)',NULL),(8006,'culture/images/maharashtra/akola/architecture/moreshwar-mandir-in-barshi-takli-highlight_w2tX7BL.jpg','Moreshwar Mandir in Barshi Takli, highlighted in vibrant yellow, green, red, orange, and pink colors, blending Hemadpanthi form with a masjid-like exterior. (Source: CKA Archives)','Moreshwar Mandir in Barshi Takli, highlighted in vibrant yellow, green, red, orange, and pink colors, blending Hemadpanthi form with a masjid-like exterior. (Source: CKA Archives)',NULL),(8009,'culture/images/maharashtra/akola/architecture/the-balapur-chhatri-stands-on-a-high-platf_tRFg7mc.jpg','The Balapur Chhatri stands on a high platform by the river, with five domes supported by twenty pillars and is built in a Rajputana style. (Source: Akola City Word Press)[2]','The Balapur Chhatri stands on a high platform by the river, with five domes supported by twenty pillars and is built in a Rajputana style. (Source: Akola City Word Press)[2]',NULL),(8013,'culture/images/maharashtra/akola/architecture/a-gateway-leading-into-balapur-fort-reflec_mPYWqgv.jpg','A gateway leading into Balapur Fort, reflecting Indo-Islamic architectural elements. (Source: CKA Archives)','A gateway leading into Balapur Fort, reflecting Indo-Islamic architectural elements. (Source: CKA Archives)',NULL),(8019,'culture/images/maharashtra/akola/architecture/the-large-door-of-the-house-has-a-space-wh_JGBbKwW.jpg','The large door of the house has a space where vegetable vendors sit and sell vegetables. The devlis on the left side of the door, provide much-needed light in the evenings for the vendors. (Source: CKA Archives)','The large door of the house has a space where vegetable vendors sit and sell vegetables. The devlis on the left side of the door, provide much-needed light in the evenings for the vendors. (Source: CKA Archives)',NULL),(8021,'culture/images/maharashtra/akola/architecture/the-intricately-designed-entrance-door-of-_VbOErGp.jpg','The intricately designed entrance door of an old structure, showcasing traditional stone masonry and detailed carvings. (Source: CKA Archives)','The intricately designed entrance door of an old structure, showcasing traditional stone masonry and detailed carvings. (Source: CKA Archives)',NULL),(8022,'culture/images/maharashtra/akola/architecture/the-house-features-several-exterior-doors-_2BdeV0X.jpg','The house features several exterior doors, each with its own detailing. One such door is flanked by ‘Konades’ on either side. Konades are small niches used to place lamps at night. (Source: CKA Archives)','The house features several exterior doors, each with its own detailing. One such door is flanked by ‘Konades’ on either side. Konades are small niches used to place lamps at night. (Source: CKA Archives)',NULL),(8023,'culture/images/maharashtra/akola/architecture/these-doors-at-the-exteriors-feature-a-dis_OMt64Nd.jpg','These doors at the exteriors feature a distinct panelled design that open differently from the typical double-leaf doors elsewhere in the house. Their construction appears more utilitarian, likely tailored for commercial access rather than domestic use. (Source: CKA Archives)','These doors at the exteriors feature a distinct panelled design that open differently from the typical double-leaf doors elsewhere in the house. Their construction appears more utilitarian, likely tailored for commercial access rather than domestic use. (Source: CKA Archives)',NULL),(8025,'culture/images/maharashtra/akola/architecture/a-window-decorated-with-an-arched-brick-fr_wgNuBk3.jpg','A window decorated with an arched brick frame, showcasing local masonry skills of the time. (Source: CKA Archives)','A window decorated with an arched brick frame, showcasing local masonry skills of the time. (Source: CKA Archives)',NULL),(8027,'culture/images/maharashtra/akola/architecture/street-view-of-the-balcony-railings-source_gjlKYVW.jpg','Street view of the balcony railings. (Source: CKA Archives)','Street view of the balcony railings. (Source: CKA Archives)',NULL),(8028,'culture/images/maharashtra/akola/architecture/view-of-the-balcony-railings-from-the-insi_ZaqIXS0.jpg','View of the balcony railings from the inside. (Source: CKA Archives)','View of the balcony railings from the inside. (Source: CKA Archives)',NULL),(8030,'culture/images/maharashtra/akola/architecture/a-traditional-roshandan-originally-designe_rtBerNq.jpg','A traditional roshandan, originally designed for ventilation and natural light, placed high on the wall to support airflow in upper levels. In this home, as with many homes in the area, it has been subtly repurposed to accommodate electrical wiring.(Source: CKA Archives)','A traditional roshandan, originally designed for ventilation and natural light, placed high on the wall to support airflow in upper levels. In this home, as with many homes in the area, it has been subtly repurposed to accommodate electrical wiring.(Source: CKA Archives)',NULL),(8032,'culture/images/maharashtra/akola/architecture/old-haud-which-is-now-covered-with-cement-_llcEJdc.jpg','Old Haud which is now covered with cement. (Source: CKA Archives)','Old Haud which is now covered with cement. (Source: CKA Archives)',NULL),(8034,'culture/images/maharashtra/akola/architecture/the-old-waste-waterway-used-for-drainage-a_uNaVVaK.jpg','The old waste waterway, used for drainage and possibly to water a traditional Parasbaug (kitchen garden). This system was common in older homes. (Source: CKA Archives)','The old waste waterway, used for drainage and possibly to water a traditional Parasbaug (kitchen garden). This system was common in older homes. (Source: CKA Archives)',NULL),(8036,'culture/images/maharashtra/akola/architecture/traditional-kitchen-spaces-with-provision-_SjjxBgF.jpg','Traditional kitchen spaces with provision for LPG Gas. (Source: CKA Archives)','Traditional kitchen spaces with provision for LPG Gas. (Source: CKA Archives)',NULL),(8037,'culture/images/maharashtra/akola/architecture/chul-claystove-historically-used-for-cooki_eo1K7vp.jpg','‘Chul’ (Claystove) historically used for cooking. (Source: CKA Archives)','‘Chul’ (Claystove) historically used for cooking. (Source: CKA Archives)',NULL),(8040,'culture/images/maharashtra/akola/architecture/front-view-of-the-house-the-front-door-is-_MIMqkJL.jpg','